WEBVTT
NOTE Cedwyn film, frame 004, en. Timings are estimates from the script until the clip is rendered.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.154
<v Nia>Bore da. Medieval Archaeology, volume forty-nine: Holbrook and Thomas, An Early-medieval Monastic Cemetery at Llandough, Glamorgan.

2
00:00:06.504 --> 00:00:13.812
<v a monk of Llandough>The old walls of the villa lie under the turf; my spade still finds their stones. Another grave today.

3
00:00:14.162 --> 00:00:27.238
<v Nia>The diggers came in nineteen ninety-four, expecting a small job. Listen: 'At an early stage it became clear that the site contained numerous burials that continued beyond the bounds of the agreed excavation area.'

4
00:00:27.588 --> 00:00:37.588
<v a monk of Llandough>Beyond the bounds, aye. Every winter takes someone, and every one of them is laid on this hill, row after row, looking down on the Ely.

5
00:00:37.938 --> 00:00:47.938
<v Nia>So they widened the trench to point two two of a hectare, and counted one thousand and twenty-six burials before work stopped in September nineteen ninety-four.

6
00:00:48.288 --> 00:00:58.673
<v Nia>Mind, the Historic Environment Record gives eight hundred and fifty-eight inhumations. Two counts, both in print. We keep both on the page until someone explains the gap.

7
00:00:59.023 --> 00:01:06.331
<v a monk of Llandough>Numbers. I know faces. The shepherd's boy. The old woman from over the water. The ground keeps taking them.

8
00:01:06.681 --> 00:01:15.912
<v Nia>Coflein, the Royal Commission's record eight-oh-two-oh-nine-oh, files the cemetery; project archive CA zero seventeen. The datasets are free online at the Archaeology Data Service.

9
00:01:16.262 --> 00:01:25.108
<v a monk of Llandough>And the church? We pray in it. Who built it first, and when, I could not swear. It was old when I came.

10
00:01:25.458 --> 00:01:39.304
<v Nia>The family's Master Timeline on the Legal Wiki puts St Dochdwy's, beside the farm, at about six-fifty, thought to be an early monastery. That's a summary, not a dig, and its evidence folder is still empty.

11
00:01:39.654 --> 00:01:52.731
<v Nia>The same volume has 'From Villa to Monastery: Llandough in Context'. The graves show a great early-medieval burial ground on the villa's hill. They don't show who ran it, its name, or any deed.

12
00:01:53.081 --> 00:01:59.619
<v a monk of Llandough>Then write us down as we were: graves above the Ely, a little church, psalms at dusk.
